• What changed: the default models for three providers moved to models that yoagent has a preset for: xai grok-4 → grok-4.7, mistral mistral-large-latest → mistral-large-4, zai glm-4-plus → glm-5.3. Each comes with a comment explaining the change for users: the old and new price, the real limits it gets instead of the guessed 4096-token output, and the fact that existing setups with no model set will switch silently. The openrouter comment now gives price as its reason ($2/$10 vs $3/$15 per M).
• The new test: every_provider_default_has_a_preset_or_a_stated_reason goes through the KNOWN_PROVIDERS list. It uses the same check (runs_on_guessed_limits) that decides whether the guessed-limits warning fires, so the test and the warning agree on what "has a preset" means. It also fails if a provider listed in the DEFAULT_WITHOUT_PRESET reason table gains a preset, or if a table row names a provider the code doesn't know.
• Running it: I ran the built binary from a folder with no config file. xai, mistral and zai started on their new defaults with no guessed-limits or Unknown-model warning. groq still shows the guessed-limits warning, as it should.
